REBOOT(2)                      P32W Unix                       REBOOT(2)

NAME
       reboot - halt or restart the machine

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int reboot(int how);

DESCRIPTION
       reboot writes every modified block in the buffer cache to
       its disk, marks every mounted filesystem clean, prints
       "P32W Unix: halted" or "P32W Unix: rebooting" on the console
       and stops the system. Processes are not warned; shutdown(1)
       does that first.

       If how has RB_NOSYNC (0x200) set, the cache is not written
       and the filesystems are not marked clean: fsck(8) -y uses
       this after repairing /, whose cached blocks are stale.

       If how has RB_REBOOT (0x100) set, the
       machine is reset: control goes to the monitor at address 0,
       as with the reset button. Otherwise the CPU halts with the
       low 8 bits of how in r1; the emulators exit with that as
       their exit status.

       Only the super-user may call it.

RETURN VALUE
       None on success. -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the super-user.

SEE ALSO
       halt(1), reboot(1), shutdown(1), sync(2), fsck(8)
